Decided that we will join our cases with Uber. Deloitte’s advice on it below.

++

Overall, Bolt's appeal is in at the UT and next is to prep skeleton arguments. Tanel and Deloitte are across it. When it comes to "whether or not join in with Uber", overall sentiment from Deloitte is that there are advantages to join. Copying below Deloitte's advice. We also have an understanding what Uber's evidence is that we could benefit from, however materially Deloitte has told us joining would increase our likelihood to win.+++Potentially, not supporting Uber could lead to a *short-term cash benefit for Bolt relative to Uber*. This is on the basis that FTT decisions are only binding towards the taxpayer and so strictly speaking, third parties cannot rely on it. However, we know from the Bolt FTT decision and the most recent FTT decision for Uber, that the Tribunal considers that the UT decision in Bolt will _be useful, if not determinative,_ in deciding Uber’s appeal. Therefore, it is likely that this benefit is only likely to last until Bolt’s UT decision at the latest. If Bolt win the UT case, this will lead to a binding decision for the sector, and both Uber and FreeNow will seek to rely on it. Based on our estimates, we assume the Bolt UT case will be heard by Q4 2024, and so the relative benefit, if any, is likely only to last until Q1, our view, in the long term, *supporting Uber with their case is overall more advantageous for Bolt.* This is because:

* Uber has broader evidence (scheduled rides, packaged transactions), all of which was not considered by the FTT in our case. However, this ultimately didn’t matter in our case as the judge adopted a high degree of generality when interpreting TOMS;
* If the UT disagrees with the FTT’s approach in looking at the case in this way, and instead looks at the evidence, our evidence is not as strong as Uber’s;
* Therefore, there is a possibility that Bolt loses its case at the UT, and if Uber has good evidence and wins, this would be a disastrous outcome and wipe out any short-term advantage gained;
* However, there are mitigation options for Bolt, for example, we are able to appeal any assessments against Bolt, which will include scheduled rides (to be set out in more detail in our note to follow).

HMRC’s tactic here is clearly to divide operators and stay other appeals in which (stronger) evidence is being presented to increase their likelihood of success. By co-operating with Uber it will increase the prospects of getting all the facts and evidence from all taxpayers before the Tribunal for determination which improves the chance of an eventual decision that gives the same ruling for all players in the market.

