h3. {color:#36b37e}Deep dive insights:{color}

* *My rider smelled inappropriately* - drivers want to reach CS agent to be unmatched with the rider.
* *My rider was under the influence of alcohol* - automation isn’t triggered as the case reaches Beehive as a Safety case (with safety severity level), although this issue is not a Safety case and it simply gets escalated to regular CS. {color:#bf2600}_Check with the Safety team why that happens._{color}
* *My rider was drinking or eating in the vehicle; My rider behaved inappropriately* - Rider rating is off, drivers point that out and ask to return the feature, also they want to be sure we take action against the rider, as they can’t rate them. {color:#bf2600}_Check what’s the status of this feature._{color}
* *My rider chose incorrect pick-up location / drop-off location / vehicle category* - Connected to other issues such as cancellation, driver score drop, editing pickup points (e.g. airport).
* *Rider didn’t answer my calls* - Connected to order cancellation, rider did not pay.
* *My rider cancelled an active trip; My rider cancelled an order; My rider didn't show up* - Mostly connected to cancellation fee and driver score drop.
* *Rider made a mess in my car* - Manual support is needed to explain compensation rules (that pictures and cleaning receipt is needed). {color:#bf2600}_Check if automation could do that._{color}

Overall, in many cases driver simply repeats the same message to the driver and that shows that the driver either just wants to talk to a human, or they want to be sure that somebody is taking action (especially when the rider rating is off).

*See comments in* {color:#0747a6}*blue*{color} *below:*



* *Commission rate*: Commission percentage that will apply per ride to the driver/fleet.
** {color:#0747a6}0% for first 3 months, then 10%{color}
* *Currency*: Usually country default if not stated otherwise
** {color:#0747a6}NOK{color}
* *Invoicing strategy*: Driver or Company. This will define to whom payouts will go, and invoices will be issued.
** {color:#0747a6}Company{color}
* *Booking fee:* Is there an active booking fee in the city? If so, do you want it to be deducted from the driver (and reflected on the commission invoice), or be a 100% driver income?
** {color:#0747a6}No booking fee{color}
* *Cash-blocking thresholds*: Defines the cash ride limits for blocking/unblocking cash rides for a driver. This can be copied from other cities, but please discuss with [{color:#b3f5ff}Billing Analytics{color}|https://taxify.atlassian.net/servicedesk/customer/portal/3/group/1240/create/806] if your market is cash-heavy.
** Max negative balance before messaging: The threshold where we will notify the driver that they are getting closer to being cash-blocked
** Max negative balance before removing cash payments: The debt threshold we allow cash rides up to. After the driver exceeds this threshold, cash orders will be disabled.
** Minimal balance to unblock cash rides: The balance threshold where we will once again unblock the driver’s cash rides.
*** {color:#0747a6}We will NOT be operating with Cash in Trondheim{color}
* *List of company_ids you want this fee calculator to be attached to*.
** {color:#0747a6}ID: 65889 (Bolt Trondheim){color}
** {color:#0747a6}And future fleets registered in Trondheim{color}

_The bonuses_ section in the FOP includes more than the bonuses we reflect on the monthly invoice.

# On the monthly invoice, we display bonuses which are netted from commission.
# On FOP, we display point 1 + other compensations, fees etc too.

So, while the invoice only displays the bonuses that are discounted from commission (which is 'commission netting'), the FOP _Bonuses_ section displays a more holistic calculation of all of their bonuses. That means the FOP Bonuses section has the bonuses on the monthly invoice + those with a separate compensation invoice.

